WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.923
Three generations of getting JSON out of a model.

2
00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:05.293
Generation one: ask nicely.

3
00:00:05.573 --> 00:00:12.123
Put “respond only with JSON matching this format” in the prompt, parse the text, and retry on failure.

4
00:00:12.403 --> 00:00:21.171
It works most of the time, which is the problem: you saw in lesson 7 how the remaining failures look (preambles, code fences, renamed keys).

5
00:00:21.451 --> 00:00:25.227
Every team that shipped this way also shipped a pile of cleanup code.

6
00:00:25.507 --> 00:00:27.875
Generation two: borrow tool calling.

7
00:00:28.155 --> 00:00:36.987
Tool calling (week 4 covers it properly) lets you describe a function with a JSON Schema for its arguments, and the model returns a structured call.

8
00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:44.200
Engineers noticed they could define a fake tool whose “arguments” were the output they wanted, then force the model to call it.

9
00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:49.259
Tool arguments came back as parsed JSON, which removed most syntax failures.

10
00:00:49.539 --> 00:00:56.259
But the arguments were not guaranteed to match the schema: a required field could still be missing or the wrong type.

11
00:00:56.539 --> 00:01:00.379
And forcing a particular tool is no longer available everywhere.

12
00:01:00.659 --> 00:01:02.003
On Claude Opus 5.

13
00:01:02.283 --> 00:01:04.117
5 and Claude Sonnet 5.

14
00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:14.957
5, a tool_choice that forces a tool ( any or a named tool ) is rejected; Anthropic’s guidance is to use structured outputs when you only wanted JSON back.

15
00:01:15.237 --> 00:01:17.819
Generation three: constrain the decoder.

16
00:01:18.099 --> 00:01:24.243
Instead of hoping the model writes valid output, the serving system makes invalid output impossible.

17
00:01:24.523 --> 00:01:30.859
Anthropic’s docs describe compiling your JSON Schema into a grammar and restricting generation to it.

18
00:01:31.139 --> 00:01:47.139
The technique is general: Willard and Louf (2023) showed how to turn a regular expression or grammar into a finite-state machine and precompute, for each state, which vocabulary tokens are allowed, so the check adds little overhead per token.
